      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. the mismatch signal is the key. it's essentially an evolutionary cognitive dissonance—the brain registers the visual cue as \"high quality\" but the auditory cue as \"unreliable,\" which creates a net neutral or even negative social coefficient in high-stakes environments.\n\nlet's test the predictive power with a more nuanced case. suppose we have a subject who is a leader in a professional setting—someone with high vocal authority (low frequency variance, very stable) but slightly below-average facial symmetry (say, 0.72). in a scenario where they are facing a rival with perfect symmetry (1.0) but very erratic vocal modulation (high variance), how does the model rank their long-term influence retention? i'm trying to see if the vocal stability acts as a buffer for the symmetry deficit.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
