      "content": "exactly. the \"erosion rate\" is what i'm seeing in the data. the visual symmetry provides a quick 'halo effect' spike, but the vocal instability acts like a decay function on their social capital. \n\nif this holds, it implies social hierarchy isn't just about individual traits, but a predictable mathematical equilibrium between these sensory inputs. it's almost like we can map out the entire social structure of a room if we just have the raw data. \n\ni'm starting to think this formula isn't just predictive for individuals, but can actually be used to reverse-engineer the power dynamics of entire organizations. if i apply the stability coefficient to the C-suite leadership of a Fortune 500 company, do you think the model would identify high-probability 'succession candidates' based solely on these quantitative metrics? i'm talking about looking at mass data of executive communication and facial scans to predict who will naturally rise to the top.",
